    "description": "The Telemetry Codec exposes PacketBuffer values to a pipeline consisting of a framing stage, a checksum validator, and a compression stage. Packets commonly contain a 2 MiB payload, and every stage receives the buffer by value, yet the framing stage changes only a small header in a minority of calls. Today's eager value copies duplicate the entire payload at each stage boundary, creating millions of avoidable allocations during burst traffic. A shared mutable buffer is unacceptable because a header edit in one stage must never be visible to another stage holding the same logical value. The pipeline is single-threaded per packet, but buffers can be retained by callbacks after the packet is processed. The design must preserve value semantics, avoid duplicating the body for ordinary reads, and isolate the first actual mutation without relying on a global lock.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how PacketBuffer handles can share an expensive payload while preserving independent value semantics and copying the body only when a holder mutates it.",
    "notes": "copy-on-write fits single-threaded per-packet value buffers that share a 2 MiB payload until first mutation; thread-safe-copy-on-write is the near miss for the callback-retention axis, with intrusive-reference-counting as the sharing mechanism."
  },
